Portscan - Coded By Smiler

Note u need libnet and libpcap - you can get them from 
http://www.packetfactory.net/libnet and ftp://ftp.ee.lbl.gov/libpcap 
respectively.

Please note that this is based on old code so the style is not completely
how I would have wished ideally, but I think its pretty easy to follow,
and, well, it works.

Note also that the '-f[ragmentation]' option does fuck all at the moment.
(It used to work but I changed the ip interface so.....now it doesnt).

There are two main modes of operation.

Sequential mode: Portscan one host at a time. Portscan supports SYN,XMAS,
  FIN standard TCP port scans. It also supports RPC style. All this does
  is "ping" lots of ports over UDP and wait for the responses. This is
  a must if you're paranoid and don't want to query the portmapper
  directly. Note you can use netmasks or input files with this mode.

Parallel mode: Send one packet to many different hosts and wait for
  responses. This is very useful for finding boxes on a subnet and also
  for searching for linux boxes with open ports. Note if you choose -sT or
  -sL or -sK you can only use one port at a time. -sP is used to test
  whether hosts are alive (by icmp_echo).


Here are the possible options:

# My personal favourite:
# To scan www.host.com ports 1-200 with XMAS style and fragmentation
portscan www.host.com -p 1-200 -sX -f

# To scan www.host.com, ports in /etc/services and simple SYN style
portscan www.host.com 

# To scan the entire subnet, ports 1-100, FIN style and fragmentation
portscan www.host.com/24 -sF -p 1-100 -f

# Ping the hosts on the subnet and log the outcome
portscan www.host.com/24 -P -o logfile

# Scan for port 143 open on Linux boxes on that subnet
portscan www.host.com/24 -sL -p 143 -o logfile

# Scan for port 53 open on all boxes from an ipfile
portscan -i ipfile -sT -p 53 -o logfile

# Scan for RPC program 100005 (mountd) on a www.host.com ports 600-1000
# over udp with fragmentation. ph33r.
portscan www.host.com -sR -R 100005 -p 600-1000 -f

